A window that asked for two views of its surface, one per eye (its own stereo
buffers, or an engine drawing the depth of its elements), and was given one is
shown flat, and the person is now told through the problems engine
(sparky-stereo-problems), on the Vulkan and the OpenGL path alike: once, as
the problem of the driver that gives one view, naming the first program that
met it, in the ten languages; the problem ends when a window gets its two
views from the same driver. Nothing is told when the pair works or when the
window never asked (ORDER 574).

QVulkanWindow gets two views: a window that asks for stereo buffers gets a
swapchain with two image layers where the surface offers them, a framebuffer
and an image view per eye (QVulkanWindow::currentFramebuffer(RightBuffer)),
and the platform hears its views, as Qt's own Vulkan swapchain does (ORDER
573). The Wayland Vulkan window's format says what its surface has once the
swapchain's maker said it.

Qt's own Vulkan swapchain keeps the window's surface when the window drops
to one view: the Vulkan stereo layer of Mesa +stereo3d24 withdraws its
declaration with the pair, so making the surface again (o556-0001) is no
longer needed, and libqt6gui6 breaks an older layer (o574-0003).
